		{
			"id": "n4ON0neM",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 340,
			"y": -680,
			"width": 573.599609375,
			"height": 325,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0q",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 665988021,
			"version": 745,
			"versionNonce": 1398946997,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1792698193694,
			"created": 1792684464943,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "question 1 : why currentTerm = currentTerm + 1.\nanswer 1 : the candidate is leading election for next term, \nso it incremented the currentTerm and requested votes.\n\nRaft uses election numbers to totally order elections \nand detect staleness.\nIt's monotonically increasing sequence.\nFollowers uses this to take a call on whether to vote\nor not.\nIf you don't increment, for one term, there are two\nelections. Each node votes once per term, so there\nwon't be votes from the nodes on same term. which\nbreaks the vote-uniqueness rule.",
			"rawText": "question 1 : why currentTerm = currentTerm + 1.\nanswer 1 : the candidate is leading election for next term, \nso it incremented the currentTerm and requested votes.\n\nRaft uses election numbers to totally order elections \nand detect staleness.\nIt's monotonically increasing sequence.\nFollowers uses this to take a call on whether to vote\nor not.\nIf you don't increment, for one term, there are two\nelections. Each node votes once per term, so there\nwon't be votes from the nodes on same term. which\nbreaks the vote-uniqueness rule.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"baseFontSize": null,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "question 1 : why currentTerm = currentTerm + 1.\nanswer 1 : the candidate is leading election for next term, \nso it incremented the currentTerm and requested votes.\n\nRaft uses election numbers to totally order elections \nand detect staleness.\nIt's monotonically increasing sequence.\nFollowers uses this to take a call on whether to vote\nor not.\nIf you don't increment, for one term, there are two\nelections. Each node votes once per term, so there\nwon't be votes from the nodes on same term. which\nbreaks the vote-uniqueness rule.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"labelPosition": null,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "hIaZ4k0D",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -500,
			"y": -680,
			"width": 616.1596069335938,
			"height": 500,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0r",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1625638485,
			"version": 967,
			"versionNonce": 712397019,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1792730829661,
			"created": 1792684584104,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "case 1: as currentTerm is incremented, what happens \na. if the candidate wins the election.\nb. if the candidate loses the election.\nc. if the election timed out.\nd. if the candidate crashes.\n\ndefence 1: \na. nothing. term will be same. candidate becomes leader.\n\nb. if the candidate loses, if the difference is just 1, in the\nnext heart beat or AppendEntries (whichever comes first),\nthe candidate checks the term and updates itself to\nthe term and get back to follower. (slide 3 and 6). or the vote\nresponse has higher term. in that case, candidate withdraws.\n\nc. couldn't find the logic. I believe the candidate still\ncontinues as a candidate.\n\nd. \n",
			"rawText": "case 1: as currentTerm is incremented, what happens \na. if the candidate wins the election.\nb. if the candidate loses the election.\nc. if the election timed out.\nd. if the candidate crashes.\n\ndefence 1: \na. nothing. term will be same. candidate becomes leader.\n\nb. if the candidate loses, if the difference is just 1, in the\nnext heart beat or AppendEntries (whichever comes first),\nthe candidate checks the term and updates itself to\nthe term and get back to follower. (slide 3 and 6). or the vote\nresponse has higher term. in that case, candidate withdraws.\n\nc. couldn't find the logic. I believe the candidate still\ncontinues as a candidate.\n\nd. \n",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"baseFontSize": null,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "case 1: as currentTerm is incremented, what happens \na. if the candidate wins the election.\nb. if the candidate loses the election.\nc. if the election timed out.\nd. if the candidate crashes.\n\ndefence 1: \na. nothing. term will be same. candidate becomes leader.\n\nb. if the candidate loses, if the difference is just 1, in the\nnext heart beat or AppendEntries (whichever comes first),\nthe candidate checks the term and updates itself to\nthe term and get back to follower. (slide 3 and 6). or the vote\nresponse has higher term. in that case, candidate withdraws.\n\nc. couldn't find the logic. I believe the candidate still\ncontinues as a candidate.\n\nd. \n",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"labelPosition": null,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "Wv88xIoE",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 160,
			"y": -140,
			"width": 800,
			"height": 800,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0s",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1468962811,
			"version": 2503,
			"versionNonce": 1543652603,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1792698573429,
			"created": 1792684647967,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "question 2: why you need all those variables to request vote\nanswer 2: nodeId -> for the follower to note to whom they voted. So that they\nwon't same leader twice.\ncurrentTerm -> for follower to update their current term.\nlog.length -> this is important. no follower want to vote for a lagging leader.\nOne way to check is by checking log length. if candidate log length is less than\nfollower -> vote is note casted.\nlastTerm -> this is tricky. this is to make sure that follower is voting for\na candidate who is at least one term ahead of him. This I believe, guarantees\nthat the candidate log is ahead of follower. an invariant Raft guarantees. \n\nwhat other invariants Raft guarantees?\n1. Leader Completeness property -> any log entry committed in a past term will\nbe present in the log of every future leader. That's the one the vote-granting\nlog check directly protects.\n2. Election Safety -> at most one leader can be elected in a given term. This\ncomes from the combination of majority quorum requirement and each node voting\nonly once per term; you mathematically can't have two disjoint majorities in the\nsame term among the same node set.\n3. Log Matching -> if two logs contain an entry with the same index and the\nsame term, then the logs aer identical in all entries up through that index. The\nleader only needs to check one prior entry to be confident everything before it\nalready matches.\n4. State Machine Safety -> If a node has applied a particular log entry at a\ngiven index to it's state machine, no other node will ever apply a different entry\nfor that same index. This is really the ultimate safety goal everything else\nserves, it's what guarantees you never get divergent application state across\nthe cluster.\n5. Leader Append Only -> a leader never overwrites or deletes entries in its own\nlog. It only ever appends. This one matters because it simplifies reasoning about\nthe leader's log as a source of truth that only grows forward.\n",
			"rawText": "question 2: why you need all those variables to request vote\nanswer 2: nodeId -> for the follower to note to whom they voted. So that they\nwon't same leader twice.\ncurrentTerm -> for follower to update their current term.\nlog.length -> this is important. no follower want to vote for a lagging leader.\nOne way to check is by checking log length. if candidate log length is less than\nfollower -> vote is note casted.\nlastTerm -> this is tricky. this is to make sure that follower is voting for\na candidate who is at least one term ahead of him. This I believe, guarantees\nthat the candidate log is ahead of follower. an invariant Raft guarantees. \n\nwhat other invariants Raft guarantees?\n1. Leader Completeness property -> any log entry committed in a past term will\nbe present in the log of every future leader. That's the one the vote-granting log check directly protects.\n2. Election Safety -> at most one leader can be elected in a given term. This comes from the combination of majority quorum requirement and each node voting only once per term; you mathematically can't have two disjoint majorities in the same term among the same node set.\n3. Log Matching -> if two logs contain an entry with the same index and the same term, then the logs aer identical in all entries up through that index. The leader only needs to check one prior entry to be confident everything before it already matches.\n4. State Machine Safety -> If a node has applied a particular log entry at a given index to it's state machine, no other node will ever apply a different entry for that same index. This is really the ultimate safety goal everything else serves, it's what guarantees you never get divergent application state across the cluster.\n5. Leader Append Only -> a leader never overwrites or deletes entries in its own log. It only ever appends. This one matters because it simplifies reasoning about the leader's log as a source of truth that only grows forward.\n",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"baseFontSize": null,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "question 2: why you need all those variables to request vote\nanswer 2: nodeId -> for the follower to note to whom they voted. So that they\nwon't same leader twice.\ncurrentTerm -> for follower to update their current term.\nlog.length -> this is important. no follower want to vote for a lagging leader.\nOne way to check is by checking log length. if candidate log length is less than\nfollower -> vote is note casted.\nlastTerm -> this is tricky. this is to make sure that follower is voting for\na candidate who is at least one term ahead of him. This I believe, guarantees\nthat the candidate log is ahead of follower. an invariant Raft guarantees. \n\nwhat other invariants Raft guarantees?\n1. Leader Completeness property -> any log entry committed in a past term will\nbe present in the log of every future leader. That's the one the vote-granting log check directly protects.\n2. Election Safety -> at most one leader can be elected in a given term. This comes from the combination of majority quorum requirement and each node voting only once per term; you mathematically can't have two disjoint majorities in the same term among the same node set.\n3. Log Matching -> if two logs contain an entry with the same index and the same term, then the logs aer identical in all entries up through that index. The leader only needs to check one prior entry to be confident everything before it already matches.\n4. State Machine Safety -> If a node has applied a particular log entry at a given index to it's state machine, no other node will ever apply a different entry for that same index. This is really the ultimate safety goal everything else serves, it's what guarantees you never get divergent application state across the cluster.\n5. Leader Append Only -> a leader never overwrites or deletes entries in its own log. It only ever appends. This one matters because it simplifies reasoning about the leader's log as a source of truth that only grows forward.\n",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"labelPosition": null,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "NgiN221c",
			"type": "text",
